# aeneid-015 draft: each unit names where it ENDS (line, closing text); t is cut from the source by aeneid_lib.cut.
TITLE = "Di aperti. Flamma Iuli"
TITLE_EN = "The Vision of the Gods and the Omen of Iulus — Aeneid II.588–688"
PART = "The Vision of the Gods and the Omen of Iulus (II.588–688)"
ABOUT = "Aeneas, still telling Dido how Troy fell, was carried along in blind fury (Greenough prints the line that closes his wrath, with the Helen passage before it, in square brackets as spurious) when his mother Venus showed herself to him, clear and shining in pure light, as the gods see her; she caught his right hand and held him back. Why this grief, she asks, and where has his care for his own gone? Let him first look where he left his aged father, and whether his wife Creusa and the boy Ascanius still live: Greek battle-lines circle them, and but for her care fire and sword would have taken them already. It is not Helen's face that he should hate, nor Paris, but the harshness of the gods that is overturning Troy. She will tear away the cloud that dulls his mortal sight; he is not to fear his mother's orders. Then she shows him the gods at work: Neptune shaking the walls from their foundations with his trident, Juno holding the Scaean gate in armour and calling the allied host from the ships, Pallas on the citadel with her storm-cloud and Gorgon, and Jupiter himself giving the Greeks courage and strength and rousing the gods against the Trojan arms. He is to flee, and she will be at his side and set him safe at his father's threshold. She vanishes into the thick shadows of night, and the dread shapes and hostile powers of the gods appear. All Ilium seemed to sink into the flames, as an ancient ash on a mountain, hacked with axes, nods and threatens, groans at last and crashes down the ridges. Aeneas descends and, a god leading him, passes through fire and foes unhurt. At his old home his father Anchises refuses to prolong his life or go into exile now that Troy is destroyed: let the young and strong take flight; had the gods wished him to live they would have kept this house for him. He will find death by his own hand, or the enemy's, and burial is a small loss; for years he has lingered, hated by the gods, since Jupiter's thunderbolt touched him. Aeneas, Creusa, Ascanius and the whole household beg him in tears not to bring everything down with himself, but he refuses and holds to his place. Aeneas, in despair, takes up arms again and longs for death. In a speech he reproaches his father and the gods: is he to leave his father behind, when Pyrrhus is on his way, soaked in Priam's blood, to kill the son before the father's eyes and the father at the altar? Was it for this that his mother rescued him, to see his father, his son and his wife slaughtered in each other's blood? Arms! The last day calls the conquered; they will not all die unavenged. He arms and is leaving the house when Creusa clings to his feet at the threshold, holding the little Iulus out to his father: if he goes to die, let him take them too; if he trusts arms, let him first guard this house; to whom are boy, father and wife left? As she fills the house with her cries, a portent comes: between the hands and faces of the grieving parents a soft tongue of flame seems to pour light from the top of Iulus' head and to play harmlessly about his hair and temples. Fearful, they beat out the blazing hair and quench the holy fire with water. But Anchises lifts his eyes to the stars with joy and, stretching his palms to heaven, begins to pray."
U = [
((588, 'ferebar:]'),
"[Such things I was flinging out, and by a maddened mind I was being borne along:]",
"[So I cried out, and a frenzied mind swept me on:]",
"A new paragraph. Greenough prints this line, the last of the stretch 567–588, in square brackets as spurious (the Helen episode). It rounds off Aeneas' angry thoughts of the lines just before. 'Talia' is the neuter plural object of 'iactabam', the imperfect of 'iacto', 'fling out, utter'. 'furiata mente' is an ablative of manner or attendant circumstance with 'ferebar'; 'furiata' is the perfect passive participle of 'furio', 'driven to frenzy'. 'ferebar' is the imperfect passive, 'I was being carried (along)'. Aeneas is still speaking to Dido."),
((593, 'ore:'),
"when to me herself, not before to my eyes so clear, to be seen\nshe offered, and in pure light through the night shone back,\nthe kind parent, confessed a goddess, and such as to be seen\nto the heaven-dwellers and as great as she is accustomed, and by the right hand seized\nshe held me, and with rosy mouth these things besides she added:",
"when my gracious mother offered herself to my sight, more clear to my eyes than ever before, and shone in pure light through the night, revealed as a goddess, in the form and stature in which she is seen among the gods; she seized me by the right hand and held me, and spoke from her rosy lips:",
"A 'cum' clause with the indicative (the 'inverse' use: the main action comes in the 'cum' clause) and the perfect 'obtulit'. 'mihi' is a dative of the person to whom, 'se… videndam' is a gerundive agreeing with 'se', 'herself to be seen' (that is, 'for me to see'). 'non ante… tam clara' is 'not before so clear', 'oculis' being a dative of reference ('to my eyes'). 'alma parens' is Venus, who is not named. 'confessa' is the perfect participle of the deponent 'confiteor', with present sense, 'having revealed herself' (you'll see this constantly), and 'deam' is a predicate accusative, 'as a goddess'. 'qualisque… et quanta': 'such and as great as she is accustomed to seem to the gods'; 'videri' is an infinitive depending on 'solet', and the correlatives 'talem tantamque' are understood. 'caelicolis' is a dative, 'to the heaven-dwellers' (the gods). 'dextra… prehensum' means that she took him 'seized by the right hand', with 'me' understood; 'continuit' is 'held back, restrained'. 'roseo… ore' is an ablative of means or source. 'haec insuper addidit' is 'added these things besides'. The speech follows, with the verb of speaking already used."),
((594, 'iras?'),
"“Son, what grief so great stirs up unconquered angers?",
"“My son, what grief so great stirs up this untamed anger?",
"Venus' speech begins, with the opening “. 'Nate' is a vocative. 'quis' is an old form of the interrogative adjective 'qui', agreeing with 'dolor', 'what grief?' (it stands for 'qui'). 'tantus dolor' is the subject, 'excitat' is 'rouses', and 'indomitas iras' is the object, 'untamed angers'; the plural 'irae' is a regular poetic plural for one emotion."),
((595, 'recessit?'),
"Why do you rage, or whither of us to you has care withdrawn?",
"Why this rage? Or where has your care for us withdrawn?",
"'quid' is an accusative of respect or extent used as an adverb, 'why?'. 'furis' is the second person singular of 'furo', 'rage, be mad'. 'quonam' is 'whither (then)?', with the emphatic -nam. 'nostri' is the objective genitive of 'nos' (A&G: 'nostri' is the genitive for the objective use, 'care for us'), and 'tibi' is a dative of reference. 'recessit' is the perfect of 'recedo'."),
((597, 'Anchisen;'),
"Not first will you look, where weary with age your parent\nyou have left, Anchises;",
"Will you not first look to where you left your father, spent with age, Anchises;",
"'Non prius aspicies' is a future used with the force of a command or an urgent question (a question expecting the answer yes: 'Will you not first look?'). 'ubi liqueris' is an indirect question, 'where you have left', with 'liqueris' a perfect form of 'linquo', 'leave'. 'fessum aetate parentem' is 'a father worn out with age' ('aetate' is an ablative of cause), and 'Anchisen' is in apposition to 'parentem', a Greek accusative singular in -en (you'll see this constantly with names)."),
((598, 'puer?'),
"whether survives your wife Creüsa,\nand Ascanius the boy?",
"whether your wife Creusa survives, and the boy Ascanius?",
"'superet' is the present subjunctive of 'supero' in the sense 'survive, be left', in an indirect question joined to the previous one, and the -ne of 'coniunxne' is the enclitic of a question, 'whether'. The verb is singular because it agrees with the nearer subject, 'Creusa'; 'Ascanius… puer' is a second subject. Greenough prints 'Creüsa' with a diaeresis, marking the three syllables (the Latin Library prints 'Creusa'; no difference of sense). Ascanius is also called Iulus (see 674)."),
((600, 'ensis.'),
"Whom all everywhere Greek\nbattle-lines around wander, and, unless my care should resist,\nalready flames would have borne off and the enemy sword would have drained.",
"All of them the Greek battle-lines are circling on every side; and if my care did not stand against them, flames by now would have swept them off, or the enemy's sword have drained their blood.",
"'Quos' is a connecting relative (you'll see this constantly): 'and all of these…'. 'omnes' is the Greenough reading, where the Latin Library has 'omnis', the same word in another spelling. 'Graiae acies' are 'Greek battle-lines', and 'circum errant' is 'wander about' (the acies are the subject). 'ni mea cura resistat' is a conditional clause with the present subjunctive; 'tulerint' and 'hauserit' are perfect subjunctives in the main clause, 'would have carried off… drained' (the pattern mixes present and perfect subjunctive). 'flammae' and 'inimicus ensis' are subjects: the first verb belongs to the flames, the second to the sword. 'hauserit' is from 'haurio', 'drain, drink up'; of a sword it means that it drinks the blood."),
((602, 'Paris:'),
"Not to you of the Tyndarid the face hateful, the Laconian woman,\nnor blamed Paris:",
"It is not the hateful face of Helen, the Spartan woman, that does this to you, nor the guilt of Paris:",
"'Tyndaridis… Lacaenae' are two genitives, 'of the Tyndarid, the Spartan woman': 'Tyndaris' (a Greek patronymic, 'daughter of Tyndareus') and 'Lacaena' ('Laconian woman') are both Helen. 'tibi… invisa' is the dative with the adjective 'invisus', 'hateful (to you)', and 'est' is understood. 'culpatus' is a perfect passive participle used as an adjective, 'blamed', and '-ve' is 'or'. Venus is saying that the human cause, Helen and Paris, is not the real one."),
((603, 'Troiam.'),
"of the gods the harshness, of the gods,\noverturns these resources and lays low from its summit Troy.",
"it is the harshness of the gods, the gods, that overturns this great power and brings Troy down from its summit.",
"'divom' is the old genitive plural in -om (you'll see this constantly); its repetition ('divom… divom') is an emphatic repetition with the subject 'inclementia' between. 'evertit' is the present of 'everto', 'overturn'. 'opes' are 'resources, power, wealth', and 'has' points to Troy's present ruin. 'sternit' is 'lays low' and 'a culmine' 'from the peak' (the height of its pride)."),
((604, 'Aspice—'),
"Look—",
"Look—",
"A dash: Venus breaks off the command 'aspice' to put in a long parenthesis (604–607), and the object of the sight comes in 608. 'Aspice' is the imperative of 'aspicio'."),
((606, 'eripiam;'),
"for all, which now drawn over to you looking\nmortal sight dulls and damp around\ndarkens, the cloud I will snatch away;",
"for I shall tear away the whole cloud that now hangs over you as you look, dulling your mortal sight and darkening around you with its damp;",
"'namque' is an emphatic 'for'. 'omnem… nubem' frame the clause (hyperbaton: 'all… cloud'), and 'nubem' is the object of 'eripiam', future of 'eripio'. 'quae' is a relative whose antecedent follows it, 'nubem'. 'obducta' is a perfect passive participle, 'drawn over', and 'tuenti' is a dative of the present participle of 'tueor', 'to you looking'. 'mortalis… visus' is the accusative plural in -is (you'll see this constantly). 'tibi' is a dative of reference, 'umida' is feminine nominative, agreeing with the cloud (the relative 'quae' is the subject of both 'hebetat' and 'caligat'), 'damp'; 'circum' is an adverb, 'caligat' the present of 'caligo', 'be dark, give darkness'. The cloud keeps mortal eyes from seeing the gods."),
((607, 'recusa:—'),
"you, let no commands of the parent\nfear, nor the instructions to obey refuse:—",
"do not be afraid of any command your mother gives, or refuse to obey her instructions:—",
"'ne… time' and 'neu… recusa' are prohibitions made with the imperative (a poetic usage; in prose 'noli timere' or 'ne timueris'); 'qua' stands for 'ulla' after 'ne'. 'parentis' is the genitive of 'parens' (the mother), 'iussa' is 'commands' (neuter plural of the participle used as a noun) and 'praeceptis' is a dative with 'parere'. 'recusa' is the imperative of 'recuso'. Greenough ends the parenthesis with a colon and a dash."),
((609, 'fumum.'),
"here, where scattered masses and torn from stones\nstones you see, and mixed with dust the billowing smoke.",
"here, where you see the masses of masonry thrown apart, stones torn from stones, and the smoke rolling up thick with dust.",
"The sentence that 'Aspice' began is finished: 'hic' (here) is the place, and the relative clause with 'ubi' describes what he sees. 'disiectas moles' are 'scattered masses' of masonry; 'avolsa saxis saxa' is 'stones torn from stones' (the repetition is a figure, and 'saxis' an ablative of separation). Greenough prints 'avolsaque' (the Latin Library 'auulsaque' is only a spelling). 'mixto… pulvere' is an ablative of accompaniment or means, and 'undantem' is 'billowing, surging in waves'."),
((612, 'eruit;'),
"Neptune the walls and from his great trident dislodged\nfoundations shakes, and the whole from its seats city\nuproots;",
"Neptune is shaking the walls and the foundations, wrenched loose by his great trident, and tearing the whole city from its seat;",
"'magno… emota tridenti' is 'dislodged by his great trident': 'emota' agrees with 'fundamenta' and 'tridenti' is an ablative of means. 'quatit' is 'shakes'. 'totamque a sedibus urbem' frames the clause, with 'a sedibus' 'from its seats, from its foundations', and 'eruit' is the present of 'eruo', 'tear up by the roots'."),
((614, 'vocat.'),
"here Juno the Scaean most savage gates\nfirst holds, and the allied raging from the ships army\nwith iron girt calls.",
"here Juno, fiercest of all, holds the Scaean gates in the front, and, raging, belted with steel, calls the allied host from the ships.",
"'hic' (here) answers Neptune's place with Juno's. 'Scaeas… portas' are the Scaean gates, a famous gate of Troy. 'saevissima' is a superlative, 'most fierce', and 'prima' a predicative adjective, 'foremost'. 'socium' is a genitive plural in -um (you'll see this constantly) for 'sociorum', 'of allies', going with 'agmen'; the host is the allied Greek army. 'ferro accincta' is 'girt with steel', the perfect passive participle used in a middle sense with an ablative of means (the participle is feminine, agreeing with Juno). The line is a short one, a half-line, and is metrically incomplete; Virgil's unfinished lines are well known."),
((616, 'saeva.'),
"Now the highest citadels Tritonian—look back—Pallas\nhas occupied, shining out with storm-cloud and with Gorgon fierce.",
"Now look back: on the topmost citadel Pallas of Triton has taken her seat, blazing out with her storm-cloud and the savage Gorgon.",
"'Tritonia' is an epithet of Pallas (Athena); 'respice' is the imperative 'look back', an exclamation to draw the eye. 'summas arces' is an accusative with 'insedit', the perfect of 'insido', which takes the accusative, 'has settled on'. 'effulgens' is the present participle, 'blazing out'; 'nimbo' and 'Gorgone saeva' are ablatives of means or manner, the Gorgon being the head she wears (on her aegis)."),
((618, 'arma.'),
"Himself the father to the Danaans courage and strengths favourable\nsupplies, himself the gods against the Dardan arms rouses.",
"The father of the gods himself supplies the Greeks with courage and favouring strength; he himself stirs the gods up against the arms of Troy.",
"'pater' is Jupiter, as 'father' of gods and men (see 648). 'Danais' is a dative, 'for the Danaans', with 'sufficit', 'supplies'. 'animos viresque secundas': 'secundas' agrees with 'vires' (which is feminine), 'favourable strength'. The Latin Library prints 'virisque' for Greenough's 'viresque', an alternative spelling of the same noun. 'in Dardana… arma' means 'against the Dardan arms'; 'Dardana' is an adjective from Dardanus, ancestor of the Trojans, thus 'Trojan'. 'ipse… ipse' repeat for emphasis (anaphora)."),
((619, 'labori.'),
"Snatch, son, flight, and an end impose on the toil.",
"Seize your escape, my son, and put an end to your toil.",
"'Eripe' is the imperative of 'eripio', 'snatch (for yourself)'; 'fugam' is the object, 'flight'. 'Nate' is a vocative. 'finem… impone' is an imperative of 'impono', 'impose'; 'labori' is a dative. The 'labor' is the long struggle."),
((620, 'sistam.”'),
"Nowhere shall I be absent, and safe on your father's threshold you I shall set.”",
"I shall be with you everywhere, and shall set you safe at your father's door.”",
"The closing ” ends Venus' speech. 'Nusquam abero' is the future of 'absum', 'I shall nowhere be away', a litotes for 'I shall be at your side everywhere'. 'tutum' is a predicative adjective, 'safe', agreeing with 'te', and 'sistam' is the future of 'sisto', 'set, place'. 'patrio… limine' is best taken as an ablative of place, 'on your father's threshold'; 'patrio' is the adjective from 'pater'."),
((621, 'umbris.'),
"She had spoken, and in the thick of night herself hid shadows.",
"She had spoken, and hid herself in the dense shadows of the night.",
"'Dixerat' is a pluperfect, 'she had spoken'. 'spissis noctis umbris' is an ablative of place where without 'in' (or of means), 'in the thick shadows of night', and 'se condidit' is 'hid herself' (perfect of 'condo')."),
((623, 'deum.'),
"Appear dread shapes and hostile to Troy\npowers great of gods.",
"Terrible shapes appear, and great divinities hostile to Troy.",
"'Adparent' is Greenough's spelling of 'apparent' (the Latin Library prints it so; the assimilated form is only a spelling), the present tense, 'there appear', with the vivid immediacy of the historic present. 'dirae facies' are 'dread forms', and 'numina magna deum' is 'great powers of the gods' ('deum' is the genitive plural in -um); 'inimica… Troiae' is 'hostile to Troy', the dative with the adjective. The second line is a short one, an unfinished half-line."),
((625, 'Troia;'),
"Then indeed all to me seemed to sink into the fires\nIlium, and from the bottom be overturned Neptunian Troy;",
"Then truly all Ilium seemed to me to be sinking into the flames, and Neptune's Troy to be overturned from its base;",
"A new paragraph. 'Tum vero' is 'then indeed'. 'visum (est)' is the perfect passive of 'video' used as 'it seemed', with the accusative and infinitive 'omne Ilium considere', and 'mihi' is the dative of the person. 'considere' is the infinitive of 'consido', 'sink down', 'in ignis' the accusative plural in -is. 'verti' is the passive infinitive, 'be overturned', and 'ex imo' 'from the very bottom'. 'Neptunia' is 'Neptune's': on the traditional account, the god had a hand in building Troy's walls (see his work on them at 610–611)."),
((628, 'certatim,—'),
"and as on the mountains' summits an ancient ash\nwhen with iron cut and with frequent double axes\npress to uproot it farmers in rivalry,—",
"and as, high on the mountains, an old ash tree, hacked with steel and with thick-falling axes, is attacked by farmers vying with each other to uproot it,—",
"The beginning of a simile: 'ac veluti… cum' is 'and as when…'. 'cum' has the indicative 'instant', and the clause is 'when the farmers press on to uproot an ash'. 'antiquam… ornum' frames the line (hyperbaton): 'ornus' is a feminine noun, so 'antiquam' is feminine. 'ferro accisam' is a perfect passive participle with an ablative of means, 'hacked with steel'; 'crebris… bipennibus' is another ablative of means, 'with frequent double-edged axes'. 'instant' takes the infinitive 'eruere', 'to uproot'. 'certatim' is an adverb, 'in rivalry, vying'. Greenough ends the clause with a comma and a dash."),
((631, 'ruinam.'),
"that ever threatens\nand shaken as to the hair with shaken crown nods,\nuntil by wounds little by little overcome, its last\nit groaned, and torn from the ridges drew its ruin.",
"it keeps threatening to fall, and, trembling, nods its leafy head with its crown shaken, until, little by little mastered by the blows, it gives one last groan and, torn from the ridges, brings its ruin down.",
"The apodosis of the simile. 'illa' is the tree. 'usque' is 'continually, all the while'. 'tremefacta comam' has an accusative of respect, 'shaken in respect of its hair (foliage)': a participle used with a retained accusative, as often in Latin poetry. 'concusso vertice' is an ablative of manner or means, 'with its shaken crown'. 'donec… evicta' has a participle where a finite verb might be expected, 'until (it was) overcome'; 'volneribus' is an ablative of means (Greenough spells it with an o, as with 'volnus'). 'supremum' is a neuter accusative used as an adverb, 'for the last time'; 'congemuit' is the perfect of 'congemo', 'groan aloud'. 'iugis' goes either with 'avolsa' ('torn from the ridges') or with 'traxit' ('dragged along the ridges'); 'ruinam trahere' is 'to draw its own fall after it'."),
((633, 'expedior;'),
"I go down, and with a god leading, the flame between and the enemies\nI am set free;",
"I go down, and, with a god as my guide, I pass out between the fire and the enemy;",
"'Descendo' is the present tense in a vivid narrative. 'ducente deo' is an ablative absolute, 'a god leading'; the god is Venus, though she is not named. 'flammam inter et hostis': 'inter' follows its two objects, 'between the flame and the enemies' (anastrophe), and 'hostis' is an accusative plural in -is. 'expedior' is the passive of 'expedio' with a middle sense, 'I am extricated, I get clear'."),
((633, 'recedunt.'),
"give weapons place, and the flames draw back.",
"the weapons give way, and the flames draw back.",
"'dant tela locum' is 'the weapons give way' (give place). The two verbs are present, the same vivid present. The unit begins in mid-line, after the semicolon."),
((638, 'pati.'),
"And when now to my ancestral seat's thresholds it was reached,\nand ancient dwellings, my father, whom to carry up to the high\nI wished first the mountains and first sought,\nrefuses, Troy destroyed, to prolong life\nand exile to endure.",
"And when we had reached the threshold of my father's house, the old home, my father, whom I had hoped first of all to carry up into the high mountains, and whom I sought first, refuses, now that Troy is destroyed, to draw out his life or to endure exile.",
"A new paragraph. 'perventum (est)' is an impersonal perfect passive (A&G: intransitive verbs form an impersonal passive), 'it was arrived', that is, 'when we had arrived'. The word 'est' is understood. 'ad limina patriae… sedis' is 'to the threshold of the ancestral seat', and 'antiquas domos' (accusative plural) 'the old house'. 'genitor' is the father, Anchises (see 597), and 'quem… optabam… petebam' is a relative clause in which 'quem' is the object of both 'tollere' ('to lift up', depending on 'optabam') and 'petebam'. 'altos montis' is an accusative plural in -is, 'in altos… montis' 'up into the high mountains'. 'primum… primumque' is a double adverb, 'first… and first'. 'abnegat' takes the infinitives 'producere' (to prolong) and 'pati' (to endure). 'excisa Troia' is an ablative absolute, 'Troy being destroyed'; the verb is the participle of 'excido' (from 'caedo'), 'cut out, raze'."),
((640, 'fugam:'),
"“You, O, to whom whole of age\n“blood, he says, and solid by their own strength stand the strengths,\nyou, drive on flight:",
"“You, O you, whose blood is whole and unspent by age, you whose strength stands solid in its own vigour, you set your flight in motion:",
"Anchises' speech begins at 638 with “; Greenough prints a second “ at the head of 639, a stray mark that we keep in the text and in the literal line but do not translate. 'Vos O' is emphatic: 'you (young) ones'. 'quibus' is a dative of possession (the 'sum' construction), 'to whom (there is)' whole blood; 'aevi' is a genitive of respect, 'whole in respect of life, of years' (or 'unimpaired in age'). 'ait' is a parenthetical 'he says', inserted into the speech (the verb of speaking comes in the middle). 'suo robore' is an ablative of manner or place, 'in their own strength'. 'solidae… stant… vires' is 'solid, firm strengths stand'. 'agitate fugam' is the imperative, 'set flight in motion' or 'carry through flight'."),
((642, 'sedes.'),
"Me if the heaven-dwellers had wished to lead life,\nthese for me they would have kept dwellings.",
"If the gods had wanted me to go on living, they would have preserved this house for me.",
"A condition contrary to fact: 'si… voluissent' is pluperfect subjunctive, answered by 'servassent' (the syncopated form of 'servavissent'), also pluperfect subjunctive. 'me… ducere vitam' is an accusative and infinitive after 'voluissent', 'had wished (that) I should lead life'. 'caelicolae' is a nominative plural (the gods). 'mihi' is a dative of advantage, 'for me'. 'has… sedes' is 'this seat, this abode'."),
((643, 'urbi.'),
"Enough once and more than enough\nwe have seen the destructions and survived a captured city.",
"Once is enough, and more than enough: we have seen Troy's destruction already and outlived the capture of our city.",
"'una' is best taken as 'once', adverbially (it could be read as 'together', but the sense 'once' fits the repetition); 'satis… superque' is 'enough and more than enough'. 'exscidia' is Greenough's spelling of 'excidia' (the Latin Library reads 'excidia'), plural of 'excidium', 'destruction'. 'captae… urbi' is a dative with 'superavimus', 'we have outlived', since the verb in this sense takes the dative. 'vidimus' and 'superavimus' are perfects."),
((644, 'corpus.'),
"Thus, O, thus laid out, having addressed my body, depart.",
"Just so, just so, address my body as it lies, and leave.",
"'Sic' is repeated in the gesture of lying down as if laid out for burial, 'O' marks the pathos. 'positum… corpus' is the object of 'adfati': 'adfati' is the nominative plural perfect participle of the deponent 'adfor', 'address', with present sense (you'll see this constantly), and the body is addressed in the last farewell. 'discedite' is an imperative, 'go away'. Greenough writes 'adfati' for the usual 'affati'."),
((645, 'inveniam;'),
"Myself by hand death I shall find;",
"I shall find death by my own hand;",
"'Ipse manu' is 'I myself by my hand', 'manu' an ablative of means; 'inveniam' is a future of 'invenio'. He means that death will come by his own hand or, as the next clause adds, by an enemy's."),
((646, 'petet;'),
"will pity me the enemy\nand spoils will seek;",
"the enemy will take pity, and will come for my spoils;",
"'miserebitur' is the future of the deponent 'misereor', 'will take pity' (it normally takes the genitive; here the object is simply left out). 'hostis' is the nominative singular, 'an enemy'. 'exuvias' are 'spoils' stripped from a corpse, 'petet' 'will seek'. The sense is that death at an enemy's hands loses little, since they will only want his armour."),
((646, 'sepulcri.'),
"easy the loss of a tomb.",
"the loss of a grave is a light thing.",
"'facilis iactura sepulcri' has no verb ('est' understood), 'the loss of burial is light'; 'sepulcri' is an objective genitive, 'loss of a tomb'. The thought is that he does not need burial."),
((649, 'igni.”'),
"Already long hated by the gods and useless the years\nI delay, from when me of gods the father and of men the king\nwith the blasts of the thunderbolt breathed on and touched with fire.”",
"For a long time now I have lingered through the years, hated by the gods and no use to anyone, ever since the father of the gods and king of men breathed on me with the blasts of his thunderbolt and touched me with fire.”",
"The closing ” ends Anchises' speech. 'iam pridem' is 'long since' and goes with the present 'demoror' (a present with 'iam pridem' is translated as a perfect continuing to now: 'have been lingering'). 'invisus divis' is 'hateful to the gods': 'divis' is a dative. 'inutilis' is also nominative, agreeing with the speaker. 'annos demoror' is 'I delay (the) years', i.e. 'I linger on through the years'. 'ex quo' is 'from the time when'. 'divom pater atque hominum rex' is Jupiter, 'father of gods and king of men'; 'divom' is a genitive plural in -om, and 'hominum' another. 'fulminis… ventis' is 'with the breath (winds) of a thunderbolt', 'adflavit' being 'breathed on' and 'contigit' 'touched'."),
((650, 'manebat.'),
"Such things he persisted in saying, and fixed he remained.",
"So he kept on speaking, and stayed fixed where he was.",
"A new paragraph. 'perstabat' is the imperfect of 'persto', 'stand firm, persist'; 'memorans' is the present participle of 'memoro', 'saying, uttering'. 'fixus' is a perfect participle used as an adjective, 'fixed' (unmoved). The imperfects describe his lasting obstinacy."),
((653, 'vellet.'),
"We on the other side poured out in tears, and wife Creüsa\nand Ascanius and all the household, that not to overturn with himself\neverything the father, and to fate pressing to lean, should wish.",
"We, on our side, dissolved in tears, my wife Creusa, Ascanius, and the whole household, begging my father not to bring everything down along with himself or add his weight to the fate that was already bearing on us.",
"'contra' is an adverb, 'on the other side'. 'effusi lacrimis' is 'poured out in tears', with an ablative of means; the participle agrees with 'nos' and the group of nouns that follows. 'ne… vellet' is a negative clause of purpose or request in the imperfect subjunctive: the verb of begging is left out (a compressed construction, as the narrative is a swift summary). 'vertere secum cuncta' is 'to overturn everything along with himself', and 'fato… urguenti incumbere' is 'to lean on fate that presses', with the dative 'fato' and the present participle in the dative. Greenough spells it 'urguenti' (the Latin Library prints 'urgenti'; a spelling only)."),
((654, 'isdem.'),
"He refuses, and in his purpose and in his dwelling clings, in the same.",
"He refuses, and stays clinging to his purpose and his place.",
"'Abnegat' is the present of 'abnego', here without an object, 'he refuses'. 'incepto' is a dative (or ablative) with 'haeret', 'clings to his undertaking', and 'sedibus… in isdem' is 'in the same seat', with 'isdem' a form of 'iisdem' (the preposition 'in' stands after its noun). The tenses are present: the narration slides into the vivid present."),
((655, 'opto:'),
"Again into arms I am borne, and death, most wretched, I desire:",
"Once more I am swept to arms, and, utterly wretched, I long for death:",
"'Rursus' is 'again'. 'in arma feror' is the present passive of 'fero', 'I am carried'. 'miserrimus' is a superlative nominative, in apposition to the subject, 'most wretched'. The colon introduces the reason, in the question that follows."),
((656, 'dabatur?'),
"for what plan or what now chance was being given?",
"for what plan was left, or what chance was now offered?",
"'nam' explains the preceding clause. 'quod' is an interrogative adjective neuter agreeing with 'consilium', 'what?'. 'dabatur' is the imperfect passive of 'do', 'was being given', 'was on offer'. The question is rhetorical."),
((658, 'ore?'),
"“Did you hope that I could carry my foot, father, with you left behind,\nand that so great a crime fell from a father's mouth?",
"“Did you hope that I could take a step away, father, and leave you behind? Has so great a wickedness fallen from a father's lips?",
"Aeneas' speech begins with “. 'Mene' is 'me' plus the question enclitic '-ne', the subject accusative of 'posse'. 'sperasti' is a syncopated perfect (for 'speravisti'). The construction is an accusative and infinitive after 'sperasti': 'me… posse efferre pedem', 'that I could bear out my foot'. 'te… relicto' is an ablative absolute, 'you being left'. 'efferre pedem' is the expression 'to step out, to go away'. 'tantum nefas' is the subject of 'excidit', 'so great an impiety', and 'patrio… ore' an ablative of separation, 'from a father's mouth'. 'excidit' is the perfect of 'excido' (from 'cado')."),
((663, 'aras.'),
"If nothing of so great a city by the gods pleases to be left,\nand this is fixed in your mind, and to Troy about to perish to add\nboth yourself and yours it pleases, open to that death the door,\nand soon will be here from much of Priam's blood Pyrrhus,\nthe son before the face of the father, the father who slaughters at the altars.",
"If the gods are pleased that nothing be left of so great a city, if this is fixed in your mind, and if it is your pleasure to add yourself and your family to a Troy that is bound to perish, the door lies open to that death, and Pyrrhus will soon be here, fresh from the blood of Priam, he who kills the son before the father's eyes, and the father at the altar.",
"Two 'si' clauses and the main clause, 'patet… ianua'. 'placet' is followed by the accusative and infinitive passive 'nihil… relinqui', 'it pleases (that) nothing be left', and 'Superis' is the dative (of the person pleased). 'ex tanta urbe' is 'of so great a city'. 'sedet hoc animo' is 'this is settled in your mind', 'sedet' being the present of 'sedeo', used of a settled decision, and 'animo' an ablative of place. 'iuvat' is impersonal, followed by the infinitive 'addere', and the object is 'te… tuosque'; 'perituraeque Troiae' is a dative with the future participle (A&G: -ura participles express what is about to happen). 'isti… leto' is a dative with 'patet', and 'isti' is a pronoun that carries a tone of 'that (death) of yours'. 'aderit' is the future of 'adsum', and 'multo Priami de sanguine' 'from the much blood of Priam'. 'natum ante ora patris, patrem… ad aras' is a chiasmus (the order object-A, object-B, object-B, object-A), and 'qui' is the relative of 'Pyrrhus'. 'obtruncat' is 'hacks down, butchers', a present for a habitual or characteristic act. Aeneas has just told Dido how Pyrrhus killed Priam at the altar."),
((667, 'cernam?'),
"This was, kind parent, what me through the weapons, through the fires\nyou snatch, that in the midst of the shrines the enemy, and that\nAscanius and my father and beside them Creüsa\nthe one in the other's blood slaughtered I should see?",
"Was this the reason, kind mother, why you snatch me through weapons and flames: that I should see the enemy in the heart of my house, and Ascanius, my father, and Creusa beside them, slaughtered, each in the blood of another?",
"'Hoc erat… quod' is 'this was (the thing) that…': 'erat' is the imperfect used for a realisation of the real state of things ('so this was it!'). 'alma parens' is a vocative, Venus. 'quod me… eripis' is a clause in the present indicative, and 'ut… cernam' is a purpose (or result) clause with the present subjunctive of 'cerno', 'I should see'. 'in mediis penetralibus' is 'in the innermost parts' of the house, where 'penetralia' are the inner sanctum; 'hostem' is the object. The second 'utque' introduces a second object: 'Ascanium patremque meum iuxtaque Creüsam' ('and beside (them) Creusa') plus the participle 'mactatos'. 'alterum in alterius… sanguine' is a reciprocal, 'one in the other's blood'. 'mactatos' is the perfect passive participle of 'macto', 'slaughtered'."),
((668, 'arma;'),
"Arms, men, bring arms;",
"Arms, men, bring arms;",
"'viri' is a vocative, 'men'. 'ferte' is the imperative of 'fero'; the repetition of 'arma' is emphatic."),
((668, 'victos.'),
"calls the last light the conquered.",
"the last light calls the conquered.",
"'lux ultima' is 'the last light', the final day. 'victos' is an accusative plural of the perfect passive participle of 'vinco', 'the conquered', object of 'vocat'. The 'call' is a call to the last battle."),
((669, 'Danais;'),
"Give back me to the Danaans;",
"Give me back to the Greeks;",
"'Reddite' is an imperative of 'reddo', and 'Danais' a dative. The sense is that he will go back into the fighting and be among the Greeks."),
((670, 'proelia:'),
"let me go over again renewed\nbattles:",
"let me go back into the renewed fighting:",
"'sinite… revisam' is 'allow (that) I revisit', with the subjunctive 'revisam' after 'sinite' with no 'ut' (a poetic usage). 'instaurata' is a perfect passive participle, 'renewed', agreeing with 'proelia'. The colon introduces the defiant cry that follows."),
((670, 'inulti.”'),
"Never all today shall we die unavenged.”",
"We shall not all die today unavenged.”",
"The closing ” ends Aeneas' speech. 'Numquam' is emphatic. 'omnes' and 'inulti' are both nominative plural, 'all', 'unavenged' (predicate); 'moriemur' is the future of the deponent 'morior'. 'hodie' is 'today'. The negative pairs 'numquam omnes' to mean 'not all of us'."),
((672, 'ferebam.'),
"Hence with iron I am girt again, and into the shield the left\nI was fitting, adjusting, and out beyond the roofs I was bearing myself.",
"Then I girt myself again with the sword, and was fitting my left arm into the shield-strap, and was making my way out of the house.",
"A new paragraph. 'accingor' is the present passive of 'accingo' in a middle sense (A&G: 'I gird myself'), 'ferro' an ablative of means. 'sinistram' is 'the left (hand)', the object of 'insertabam', the imperfect of 'inserto', a frequentative of 'insero', 'thrust in, again and again'; 'clipeo' is the dative with it, 'into the shield (strap)'. 'aptans' is 'fitting'. 'me extra tecta ferebam' is 'I was carrying myself outside the roofs'. The mixture of present 'accingor' and imperfects is the narrator's vivid slide."),
((674, 'Iulum:'),
"Lo, however, clasping my feet on the threshold, my wife\nclung, and the little one to the father held out Iulus:",
"And lo, my wife clung to my feet at the threshold, holding up little Iulus to his father:",
"'Ecce autem' is 'behold, however'. 'complexa' is the perfect participle of the deponent 'complector' with present sense (you'll see this constantly), taking the object 'pedes'. 'coniunx' is Creusa. 'haerebat' is 'was clinging'. 'parvum… Iulum' is the object of 'tendebat', 'held out', with the dative 'patri'. 'Iulus' is Ascanius' other name. The colon introduces her speech."),
((675, 'tecum;'),
"“If to die you go away, also us snatch into all with you;",
"“If you are going out to die, take us with you into everything;",
"Creusa's speech begins with “. 'periturus' is the future participle, 'about to perish, intending to die'. 'abis' is the present of 'abeo'. 'et nos rape' is 'snatch us too': 'rape' is the imperative of 'rapio'. 'in omnia' is 'into all (dangers)', and 'tecum' is 'with you'."),
((677, 'domum.'),
"but if some tried hope you place in the arms taken up,\nthis first defend house.",
"but if you place any hope in the arms you have taken up, defend this house first.",
"'sin' is 'but if', and 'aliquam… spem' 'some hope'. 'expertus' is the perfect participle of the deponent 'experior', 'having tried, having had experience', with present sense (you'll see this constantly); read with 'sumptis… armis' (an ablative with 'in', 'in the arms taken up') it suggests that his trust rests on weapons proved by use. 'ponis' is the present of 'pono', 'place'. 'tutare' is the imperative of the deponent 'tutor', 'protect'; 'hanc… domum' is the object, 'this house'. 'primum' is an adverb, 'first'."),
((678, 'relinquor?”'),
"To whom little Iulus,\nto whom the father and the wife once yours called am I left?”",
"To whom are little Iulus, your father and I—once called your wife—left?”",
"The closing ” ends Creusa's speech. 'Cui… cui' repeats the dative in anaphora, 'to whom?'. The verb 'relinquor' is a first person singular passive of 'relinquo', 'am left', agreeing with the speaker herself; the subjects 'parvus Iulus' and 'pater' are loosely joined to it, rather than made the subject of a plural verb. 'coniunx quondam tua dicta' is 'a wife once called yours' (the participle 'dicta' agrees with 'coniunx'), 'quondam' meaning 'once, formerly'."),
((680, 'monstrum.'),
"Such things shouting with groaning the whole house she was filling,\nwhen sudden and wonderful to tell arises a portent.",
"Crying such things, she filled the whole house with her wailing, when a portent arose, sudden and wonderful to tell.",
"A new paragraph. 'vociferans' is the present participle of 'vocifero', 'shout aloud', agreeing with Creusa. 'gemitu' is an ablative of means, 'tectum omne' 'the whole building'. 'replebat' is the imperfect, 'was filling'. 'cum' with the indicative 'oritur' is the inverse construction (the new event is put in the 'cum' clause). 'subitum dictuque mirabile monstrum': 'dictu' is the supine in -u (A&G: ablative of respect), 'wonderful to tell'; '-que' joins the two adjectives."),
((684, 'pasci.'),
"For between the hands and the faces of the sorrowing parents\nbehold light from the top of the head of Iulus seemed\nto pour light a point of fire, and by touch harmless soft\nto lick the hair flame and around the temples to feed.",
"For between the hands and the grieving faces of his parents, look, a light point of fire seemed to pour its radiance from the crown of Iulus' head, and a flame, soft and harmless to the touch, to lick his locks and graze about his temples.",
"A new paragraph. 'Namque' explains the 'monstrum'. 'manus inter… ora' is an anastrophe, 'between the hands and the faces', with 'maestorum… parentum' a genitive, 'of the sorrowing parents'. 'levis… apex' is 'a light point' (a tip of flame). 'visus' is the perfect passive participle of 'video', 'seemed', agreeing with 'apex'; 'fundere' is the infinitive, 'pour'. The second subject, 'flamma', goes with a 'visa' understood, and the loose building of the sentence has the two phrases describe one wonder. 'tactu innoxia' has the ablative of respect, 'harmless to the touch'. 'lambere' is 'lick', and 'pasci' is the passive infinitive of 'pasco', with a middle sense, 'to feed, to graze' (the flame feeds on the hair). 'Iuli' is the genitive of 'Iulus'."),
((686, 'ignis.'),
"We fearful to tremble with fear, and the hair blazing\nto shake out and the holy to extinguish with springs fires.",
"We, in terror, tremble; we beat out the blazing hair and quench the sacred fire with water from the springs.",
"The three infinitives 'trepidare', 'excutere' and 'restinguere' are historical infinitives (A&G: used for vivid narration, with the subject in the nominative: 'nos pavidi'). 'metu' is an ablative of cause. 'crinem flagrantem' is 'the blazing hair', and 'excutere' 'to shake out'. 'sanctos… ignis' is an accusative plural in -is, 'holy fires', holy because it is a sign from the gods. 'fontibus' is an ablative of means, 'with spring-water'."),
((688, 'tetendit:'),
"But the father Anchises his eyes to the stars glad\nlifted, and to heaven palms with voice stretched:",
"But my father Anchises, glad at heart, raised his eyes to the stars, and stretched his hands to heaven with his voice:",
"'laetus' is a predicative adjective, 'glad, joyful': he reads the portent as a good sign. 'oculos ad sidera… extulit' is the perfect of 'effero', 'lifted'. 'caelo' is a dative of direction, a poetic use for 'ad caelum'. 'palmas' are the 'palms', the hands, stretched out in prayer. The colon introduces his prayer, which runs into the next part."),
]
